1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:04,600
At an esports booth the line is long, the Wi-Fi is bad, and the machine runs all day.

2
00:00:05,500 --> 00:00:09,000
Read the call. Click the spot.

3
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:23,000
Stage 1, WARM-UP: the zone labels are on.

4
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:45,000
Stage 2, LIVE: the labels fade away. You have to know the map.

5
00:00:45,000 --> 00:01:07,800
Stage 3, CLUTCH: half the calls name two spots, in order.

6
00:01:08,800 --> 00:01:13,300
Your score is a reaction time.

7
00:01:13,300 --> 00:01:17,800
Three initials, and your row appears on the second monitor. No server. (The other rows are sample scores.)

8
00:01:17,800 --> 00:01:29,800
The next player gets a clean round, with nothing left over from the last one.

9
00:01:30,800 --> 00:01:43,800
1000 rounds in one page, no reload: heap +0.21 MB, 16 DOM nodes every time, 0 errors, game work per frame 1.5 ms (p95).

10
00:01:44,600 --> 00:01:48,100
Before: WebStorm's headless inspection, run on commit 4cae2a4, flags 1 warning.

11
00:01:48,500 --> 00:01:54,600
The fix is a real diff: no more throw caught locally, one new test.

12
00:01:55,000 --> 00:01:58,000
After: 0 errors, 0 warnings. Junie CLI and Air: 0 commits so far.

13
00:01:58,800 --> 00:02:05,800
Free to play, MIT licensed. Open it once with Wi-Fi, then it works offline.
